WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.340
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:09.440
Hold on to your papers because this is an
exclusive look at a new neural network visualization

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.430
paper that came from a collaboration between
Google and the Carnegie Mellon University.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:20.420
The paper is as fresh as it gets because this
is the first time I have been given an exclusive

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.690
look before the paper came out, and this means
that this video and the paper itself will

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.130
be published at the same time.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:31.850
This is really cool and it's quite an honor,
thank you very much!

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.449
Neural networks are powerful learning-based
tools that are super useful for tasks that

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:40.499
are difficult to explain but easy to demonstrate.

00:00:40.499 --> 00:00:45.109
For instance, it is hard to mathematically
define what a traffic sign is, but we have

00:00:45.109 --> 00:00:47.219
plenty of photographs of them.

00:00:47.219 --> 00:00:51.909
So the idea is simple, we label a bunch of
photographs with additional data that says

00:00:51.909 --> 00:00:58.110
"this is a traffic sign", and "this one isn't",
and feed this to the learning algorithm.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:03.320
As a result, neural networks have been able
to perform traffic sign detection at a superhuman

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.390
level for many years now.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:10.190
Scientists at Google DeepMind have also shown
us that if we combine a neural network with

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:15.260
reinforcement learning, we can get it to look
at a screen and play computer games on a very

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:16.330
high level.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:21.840
It is incredible to see problems that seemed
impossible for many decades crumble one by

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.110
one in quick succession over the last few
years.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:28.710
However, we have a problem, and that problem
is interpretability.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:34.090
There is no doubt that these neural networks
are efficient, however, they cannot explain

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:38.160
their decisions to us, at least not in a way
that we can interpret.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.310
To alleviate this, earlier works tried to
visualize these networks on the level of neurons,

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:49.790
particularly, what kinds of inputs make these
individual neurons extremely excited.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:55.250
This paper is about combining previously known
techniques to unlock more powerful ways to

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.150
visualize these networks.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:03.230
For instance, we can combine the individual
neuron visualization with class attributions.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:08.300
This offers a better way of understanding
how a neural network decides whether a photo

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.730
depicts a labrador or a tiger cat.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:17.170
Here, we can see which part of the image activates
a given neuron and what the neuron is looking

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.170
for.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:23.370
Below, we see the final decision as to which
class this image should belong to.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:30.049
This next visualization technique shows us
which set of detectors contributed to a final

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.880
decision, and how much they contributed exactly.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.989
Another way towards better interpretability
is to decrease the overwhelming number of

00:02:57.989 --> 00:03:02.060
neurons into smaller groups with more semantic
meaning.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:07.689
This process is referred to as factorization
or neuron grouping in the paper.

00:03:07.689 --> 00:03:12.870
If we do this, we can obtain highly descriptive
labels that we can endow with intuitive meanings.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:18.599
For instance, here, we see that in order for
the network to classify the image as a labrador,

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:25.279
it needs to see a combination of floppy ears,
doggy forehead, doggy mouth, and a bunch of

00:03:25.279 --> 00:03:26.279
fur.

00:03:26.279 --> 00:03:30.620
And we can also construct a nice activation
map to show which part of the image makes

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.139
our groups excited.

00:03:32.139 --> 00:03:34.780
Please note that we have only scratched the
surface.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:39.999
This is a beautiful paper, and it has tons
of more results available exactly from this

00:03:39.999 --> 00:03:43.749
moment, with plenty of interactive examples
you can play with.

00:03:43.749 --> 00:03:49.769
Not only that, but the code is open sourced,
so you are also able to reproduce these visualizations

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:51.590
with little to no setup.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:54.109
Make sure to have a look at it in the video
description.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.040
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

